{"causes": ["The inherent dynastic rivalries within the powerful Komnenian family fostered an environment of constant internal competition for the Byzantine throne.", "Andronikos Komnenos's personal ambition, coupled with his military experience, fueled his desire to challenge his cousin, Emperor John II Komnenos.", "The Byzantine system of succession, which lacked clear primogeniture, encouraged various princes and high-ranking nobles to seek the imperial crown through intrigue and force."], "effects": ["Andronikos Komnenos's execution in 1142 definitively secured Emperor John II Komnenos's position against a significant internal dynastic challenge.", "The swift suppression of his conspiracy served as a powerful deterrent, discouraging other high-ranking Komnenian relatives from actively plotting against the emperor for a period.", "His death contributed to the consolidation of imperial power under John II, enabling him to pursue his military and diplomatic objectives with greater internal stability."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "komnenian-dynasty", "sourceName": "Komnenian Dynasty", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "andronikos-komnenos", "targetName": "Andronikos Komnenos", "context": "The Komnenian Dynasty provided Andronikos Komnenos with his noble status and the inherent expectation of political influence within the Byzantine Empire."}, {"sourceSlug": "andronikos-komnenos", "sourceName": "Andronikos Komnenos", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "constantinople-byzantine-empire", "targetName": "Constantinople, Byzantine Empire", "context": "Andronikos Komnenos's political intrigues and eventual execution took place within the imperial capital of Constantinople during the 12th century."}, {"sourceSlug": "john-ii-komnenos", "sourceName": "John II Komnenos", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "andronikos-komnenos", "targetName": "Andronikos Komnenos", "context": "Emperor John II Komnenos ordered the arrest and execution of Andronikos Komnenos following the discovery of his conspiracy in 1130 and 1142 respectively."}, {"sourceSlug": "byzantine-imperial-succession", "sourceName": "Byzantine Imperial Succession", "verb": "FRAMES", "targetSlug": "andronikos-komnenos", "targetName": "Andronikos Komnenos", "context": "The fluid and often violent nature of Byzantine imperial succession provided the context and motivation for Andronikos Komnenos's attempt to seize the throne."}, {"sourceSlug": "alexios-i-komnenos", "sourceName": "Alexios I Komnenos", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "andronikos-komnenos", "targetName": "Andronikos Komnenos", "context": "Andronikos Komnenos was the nephew of Emperor Alexios I Komnenos, whose establishment of the Komnenian dynasty set the stage for subsequent dynastic rivalries."}], "places": [{"name": "Constantinople, Byzantine Empire", "role": "Center of Imperial Intrigue"}, {"name": "Serbia", "role": "Area of Conspiratorial Alliance"}, {"name": "Hungary", "role": "Area of Conspiratorial Alliance"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Andronikos Komnenos", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "andronikos-komnenos"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1227872", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andronikos_Komnenos_(son_of_John_II)"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Andronikos Komnenos's failed conspiracy and subsequent execution were crucial in solidifying Emperor John II Komnenos's reign, allowing him to effectively govern and pursue foreign policy without major internal dynastic threats.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-09-22T07:02:55Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#35694828514", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Andronikos Komnenos, Latinized as Andronicus Comnenus, was a Byzantine prince of the Komnenian dynasty.", "newValue": "Andronikos Komnenos, a scion of the powerful Komnenian dynasty and nephew of Emperor Alexios I Komnenos, was a prominent Byzantine prince whose military career and political ambitions ultimately led to his execution in 1142. Born into the imperial purple, his life was intertwined with the intricate court politics and dynastic rivalries characteristic of 12th-century Byzantium, specifically during the reign of his cousin, Emperor John II Komnenos. His attempts to seize power represent a critical internal challenge to John II's authority, highlighting the fragility of imperial succession even within a strong ruling family.

Initially, Andronikos distinguished himself in various military campaigns, serving the Byzantine Empire with a degree of competence against formidable foes such as the Pechenegs and Seljuk Turks. However, his military prowess was overshadowed by a deep-seated ambition for the imperial throne. This desire culminated in a comprehensive conspiracy in 1130, where he sought to depose John II. He meticulously cultivated alliances with various disaffected Byzantine nobles, including his own brother, as well as foreign powers like the Serbs and Hungarians, planning a coordinated coup. The plot was uncovered, leading to his arrest, imprisonment, and eventual execution by blinding and strangulation in 1142.

The swift and decisive action taken against Andronikos Komnenos by Emperor John II had profound consequences for the stability of the Komnenian succession. His elimination solidified John II's grip on the throne, removing a significant internal threat and allowing the emperor to focus his energies on external campaigns and administrative reforms without fear of a major dynastic challenge. This episode served as a stark warning to other ambitious relatives, contributing to a period of relative internal peace that enabled the empire to regain some of its former strength under John II and his successor, Manuel I Komnenos."}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-22T07:02:55Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#35694828514", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1108, Constantinople"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "1108, Constantinople"}], "newValue": ["The inherent dynastic rivalries within the powerful Komnenian family fostered an environment of constant internal competition for the Byzantine throne.", "Andronikos Komnenos's personal ambition, coupled with his military experience, fueled his desire to challenge his cousin, Emperor John II Komnenos.", "The Byzantine system of succession, which lacked clear primogeniture, encouraged various princes and high-ranking nobles to seek the imperial crown through intrigue and force."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-22T07:02:55Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#35694828514", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1142"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1142"}], "newValue": ["Andronikos Komnenos's execution in 1142 definitively secure